  pass around — and they have opposite constraints.

  - [1mAuthenticated identity[0m (the server's ID for the account, issued at login). Buys you tracking that says who someone
    actually is, and it's the same value your backend uses, so logs join up. Costs: persisting it in the browser means
    storing an identity claim the client can edit. That's fine for labeling logs, and it is [3mnot[0m fine as the thing any
    endpoint trusts to decide who's asking — that has to stay server-side. Choosing this commits us to keeping that line
    drawn.
  - [1mClient-generated tracking handle[0m (a random ID minted in the browser, persisted, attached to events). Buys you
    cross-form correlation immediately with no API dependency and no security weight — nothing breaks if a user clears
    or edits it. Costs: it tells you a session's actions belong together, not who the person is. You'd link it to a real
    user later by logging the pairing once at login.
  - [1mBoth[0m — mint a handle for correlation, and stamp the server's user ID onto it once login succeeds. Buys the full
    picture. Costs: two concepts to keep straight, and more surface than a small app may need.

  My recommendation is the third, built in that order: the handle is the part that genuinely needs to persist and be
  readable by every form, and it works today against your stubbed [38;5;153mlogin[39m. The account ID only becomes real when [38;5;153mlogin[39m
  stops being a stub and actually calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, so it slots in later without redesign. Picking identity-only now
  means building a store whose value stays [38;5;153mundefined[39m until the API exists.

  The persistence mechanism is cheap to change later; what's expensive to change is whether other code is allowed to
  [3mtrust[0m this value.
